WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.000
On April 8th 2022, the European Union

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.600
issued a fifth round of sanctions
against Russia.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.640
"And, ladies and gentlemen,
I think that measures

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.679
on oil, and even gas,

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:17.840
will also be needed sooner or later."

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.880
Did you get that?

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.720
“Even gas will also be
needed sooner or later”

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.480
In other words, not now.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.960
Since the war in Ukraine began,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.840
the EU has imposed sanctions
that restrict the flow

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.520
of Russian money, and goods...

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.880
they’ve targeted banks, companies, individuals...

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.480
But natural gas remains untouched.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.321
The EU gets nearly a quarter
of its energy from gas.

00:00:43.321 --> 00:00:48.080
And nearly half of it comes from Russia:
the world's largest gas exporter.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.080
Russia delivers the vast majority of its gas to
Europe through this network of pipelines.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.760
And as the EU’s largest economy, nobody
buys more of it than Germany.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.640
Since the invasion of Ukraine began,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.600
Germany has paid Russia about 220 million
euros a day for gas.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.680
Germany uses gas to heat
more than 20 million homes.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.160
And to power a lot of the country’s industry.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.000
And Germany’s government has
spoken out against sanctions

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.440
that would limit the flow of it.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.320
"It's not possible...to cut the gas supplies."

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.520
But with every payment Germany makes to Russia’s
state-owned natural gas company...

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.880
it’s also paying for Russia's war.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.400
So, how did Germany become so
dependent on Russia for something

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.757
as vital as their energy supply?

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.457
And why can’t they quit?

00:01:49.245 --> 00:01:52.092
Germany was at the literal center
of the Cold War.

00:01:52.092 --> 00:01:56.466
After World War II, a fortified border
separated independent West Germany

00:01:56.466 --> 00:01:59.040
from East Germany and other Soviet
satellite states.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.832
WWII had left Germany in ruins.

00:02:02.832 --> 00:02:08.546
But by the 1950s West Germany was
experiencing a remarkable economic recovery.

00:02:08.546 --> 00:02:11.409
German industries like steel prospered.

00:02:11.409 --> 00:02:14.320
But they needed more energy to power
their growing economy.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.386
Meanwhile, over here in western Siberia

00:02:18.386 --> 00:02:21.840
the Soviet Union had just discovered
huge natural gas reserves.

00:02:23.266 --> 00:02:26.638
They had a network of pipelines to supply
major Soviet cities,

00:02:26.638 --> 00:02:30.352
but extending their pipelines to potential
customers in Europe

00:02:30.352 --> 00:02:32.748
would be a huge infrastructure project.

00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:38.155
Then, in 1969, West Germany elected
a chancellor with

00:02:38.155 --> 00:02:41.009
a new foreign policy called Ostpolitik,

00:02:41.009 --> 00:02:44.880
focused on bringing the two sides closer together
through dialogue and deals.

00:02:45.856 --> 00:02:48.521
Energy provided a great opportunity.

00:02:48.521 --> 00:02:51.200
And West Germany and the Soviet Union
struck a deal.

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:55.359
The Soviet Union would supply West Germany
with natural gas.

00:02:55.359 --> 00:02:59.409
And in return, West Germany would provide
high quality steel pipes

00:02:59.409 --> 00:03:00.900
to extend the pipelines.

00:03:01.653 --> 00:03:03.840
It was a major 20-year deal.

00:03:06.344 --> 00:03:08.315
To get how this deal locked Germany in,

00:03:08.315 --> 00:03:12.292
it’s important to understand what makes 
piped natural gas different

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:13.731
from other energy sources. 

00:03:15.443 --> 00:03:18.529
Natural gas, with coal and oil, 
is one of the three

00:03:18.529 --> 00:03:21.380
main fossil fuels used around the world.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.426
But unlike coal and oil, which can
be shipped or rerouted worldwide,

00:03:25.426 --> 00:03:30.137
piped natural gas is a regional product that
depends on proximity.

00:03:30.137 --> 00:03:33.506
To transport it, gas producers spend 
millions of dollars

00:03:33.506 --> 00:03:36.756
to build pipelines that connect 
producers and buyers.

00:03:36.756 --> 00:03:40.386
Because these pipelines are such
big and permanent commitments,

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:44.916
gas deals can link a buyer’s energy infrastructure
to the sellers for decades.

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:50.000
By the 80s, the Soviet Union built this 
pipeline network to Europe.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.773
And by the 90s, it was supplying Germany 
with 40% of its gas.

00:03:54.773 --> 00:03:57.164
Then, the Soviet Union collapsed.

00:03:59.329 --> 00:04:04.774
Russia’s state-owned corporation, Gazprom,
took over the old Soviet gas pipelines.

00:04:04.774 --> 00:04:07.190
But, the map had been redrawn.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:11.087
Russia's main pipelines now ran
through a newly independent Ukraine,

00:04:11.087 --> 00:04:16.230
putting a key part of their gas infrastructure
on land they no longer controlled.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:21.460
So, in order to diversify the routes to Germany,
Russia began building new ones.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:26.163
In 1999, they finished this pipeline
that ran through Belarus.

00:04:26.163 --> 00:04:30.080
And in 2005 they began building 
the Nord Stream pipeline

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.605
along the Baltic Sea to reach Germany directly.

00:04:33.605 --> 00:04:35.923
They also built pipelines inside Germany.

00:04:35.923 --> 00:04:40.200
And opened a subsidiary there to operate 
gas storage facilities.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.756
Including this one here, one of the largest
in Western Europe.

00:04:44.756 --> 00:04:47.315
Russia now had three
routes reaching Germany,

00:04:47.315 --> 00:04:51.527
as well as pipelines and storage
facilities inside Germany.

00:04:51.527 --> 00:04:53.371
The gas trade was strong.

00:04:53.371 --> 00:04:57.076
But it had also changed Russia's
relationship with Europe.

00:04:58.483 --> 00:05:03.653
At the end of 2008, gas price negotiations
between Russia and Ukraine fell apart.

00:05:03.653 --> 00:05:08.598
A few days later, Russia cut off gas 
to Ukraine for 20 days.

00:05:08.598 --> 00:05:12.260
The thing is, because Ukraine was 
a major transit country,

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:17.562
when Russia cut off their gas, they cut off
a lot of European gas too.

00:05:17.562 --> 00:05:20.833
As a result, all these countries saw a drop
in their supply,

00:05:20.833 --> 00:05:23.447
and tens of thousands lost heat.

00:05:23.447 --> 00:05:27.138
In Poland, at least eleven people 
froze to death.

00:05:27.138 --> 00:05:29.224
All this put Europe on alert.

00:05:29.224 --> 00:05:34.449
It was now clear that through gas flows,
Russia held immense power over Europe.

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:39.076
But, up here, another link to Russia was in
the works: Nord Stream 2.

00:05:39.076 --> 00:05:43.720
A new 11 billion dollar pipeline to run alongside
the first Nord Stream

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.773
and double the capacity to Germany.

00:05:46.773 --> 00:05:52.915
Then, in 2014, Russia annexed Crimea 
and invaded Eastern Ukraine.

00:05:52.915 --> 00:05:56.217
In response, the EU issued a series of sanctions.

00:05:56.217 --> 00:05:59.093
Some countries began to wean
themselves off Russia’s gas.

00:05:59.687 --> 00:06:02.080
But Russian gas kept flowing to Germany.

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:07.951
In fact, Germany imported more gas 
than ever before.

00:06:11.413 --> 00:06:15.834
Today, as Russia’s atrocities in Ukraine
continue to shock the world,

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.910
pressure on Germany is mounting.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:21.222
But replacing Russia’s gas isn’t easy.

00:06:21.222 --> 00:06:24.887
Because it’s been piped to homes
and businesses for decades,

00:06:24.887 --> 00:06:28.537
without a major infrastructure overhaul, 
Russian natural gas

00:06:28.537 --> 00:06:32.438
can only be replaced
with other natural gas.

00:06:32.438 --> 00:06:35.721
And Germany’s options for that are limited.

00:06:35.721 --> 00:06:41.091
The largest European natural gas reserve, here
in the Netherlands, is closing this year.

00:06:41.091 --> 00:06:44.832
Gas from Algeria and Libya is increasingly
used in those countries.

00:06:44.832 --> 00:06:48.740
Most of what is pumped to Europe 
goes to Italy and Spain.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.483
And a southern gas corridor connecting 
Azerbaijan to Europe

00:06:52.483 --> 00:06:55.937
is not transporting as much as expected.

00:06:55.937 --> 00:06:59.270
The other option is Liquified Natural Gas
or LNG.

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:02.264
That’s gas that’s been cooled until it
becomes liquid

00:07:02.264 --> 00:07:04.706
and can be transported in these massive ships

00:07:04.706 --> 00:07:06.931
from anywhere in the world.

00:07:06.931 --> 00:07:09.683
But It’s a time-consuming and expensive
alternative

00:07:09.683 --> 00:07:12.584
that requires a lot of new infrastructure.

00:07:12.584 --> 00:07:15.443
In the last two decades,
European countries have built

00:07:15.443 --> 00:07:17.620
LNG terminals along 
their coasts.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:21.081
Germany plans to open 3 
in the next 5 years.

00:07:21.081 --> 00:07:24.262
But as of today, doesn't have any. 

00:07:24.262 --> 00:07:29.367
Replacing natural gas and all fossil fuels
with renewables is Germany’s ultimate goal,

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:32.740
but that requires a massive 
and expensive transition

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:36.432
that won’t be complete until 2035.

00:07:36.432 --> 00:07:40.656
Meanwhile, at home, Germans
are demanding action.

00:07:40.656 --> 00:07:45.369
According to this poll, the majority of Germans
support a boycott of Russian gas.

00:07:45.369 --> 00:07:49.900
But economists predict that cutting gas imports
could cause an economic recession

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.727
that could cost hundreds of thousands of jobs.

00:07:52.727 --> 00:07:55.386
But they have taken some steps.

00:08:04.753 --> 00:08:07.694
The Nord Stream 2 pipeline was canceled.

00:08:07.694 --> 00:08:11.566
They have also reduced dependence
on Russia's gas by 15%.

00:08:11.566 --> 00:08:16.548
And have taken over the Gazprom subsidiary
that runs gas operations in Germany.

00:08:16.548 --> 00:08:18.553
But the government and business leaders

00:08:18.553 --> 00:08:22.585
continue to push back against
sanctioning gas entirely.

00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:24.762
Germany is stuck.

00:08:24.762 --> 00:08:28.072
And the stakes are now higher than ever.

